1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
 In this video, I'll be talking about the switch statement.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:12,000
 We'll cover basic usage, case lists, and follow-through behavior.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
 First, a little bit about switch.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
 Switch is used to easily check multiple conditions.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,000
 And it's an alternative way to do if else blocks.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:28,000
 Switch is execute from top to bottom, and they optionally have a default action.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,000
 Let's look at a basic example.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
 On the left, we have an x, and that's been assigned to the value of 3.

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 We then use the switch keyword, and we switch on x.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
 Next, we have the case keyword.

11
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
 In the case that x is equal to 1, we'll print out 1.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
 In the case that x is equal to 2, we'll print out 2.

13
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
 And in the case that x is equal to 3, we'll print out 3.

14
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
 We then have the default case.

15
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 So whenever the case is not covered, instead we'll execute the default.

16
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
 And the default case for this particular switch is to print out other and the value of x.

17
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
 It's always a good practice to have a default case, and that's just in the event that you forget to check for a case.

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
 At least you have something that will it be executed.

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
 We're not limited just to switching on integers.

20
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:22,000
 We can also switch on strings.

21
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:29,000
 So around the right, we have a string example.com, and we're switching on this URL variable that we created right here.

22
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 So we can do the case of example.com, we'll print out test.

23
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
 In the case of Google.com, we'll print out live.

24
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 And in any other case, so the default case is any other case, we'll print out dev.

25
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
 We can also have conditional cases.

26
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 So in this particular switch statement, we're creating an assigning a variable, and then we're going to switch on it.

27
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
 So we're creating a variable named result using create an assign.

28
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
 We're assigning the value of calculate and the function argument of five.

29
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:10,000
 We then use a semicolon, our curly braces, and then within those curly braces, we have our cases.

30
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:18,000
 So our first case will execute when result is greater than 10, our second case will be when our result is equal to six, then this will execute.

31
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 And the last case, if the result is less than 10, then this one will execute.

32
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
 We can also have case lists.

33
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 So case lists allows us to check for multiple things in the same case.

34
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 So if we switch on x, which would be an integer, on this first case, if it's either one, two, or three, we'll execute some code.

35
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 In the second case, if it's 10, 20, or 30, we'll execute this code.

36
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:56,000
 Case lists are useful if you want to take the same action for multiple different values.

37
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:03,000
 The default behavior for switches and go is to automatically stop executing once one of the cases matches.

38
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,000
 What this means is if we have a letter and it's a space, then this case does nothing, and none of these will be checked.

39
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:16,000
 However, if we use the fall through keyword, then automatically the next case will be executed.

40
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
 So for example, if we have the letter be equal to I, this case will be executed because I is present here.

41
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
 We'll print out a vowel.

42
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:34,000
 The fall through keyword is encountered, and we're going to automatically execute the next case, even though it's not equal to any of these.

43
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
 So we'll print out a vowel and vowels are great.

44
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:47,000
 The fall through behavior can be useful if you want to always execute some code regardless of which case was matched.

45
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:48,000
 Let's recap.

46
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 Switch can be used to easily check a variable for different values.

47
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
 You can use commas to check multiple values on a single case.

48
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,000
 Expressions are also allowed as a case.

49
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
 You can include function calls, mathematical operations, and logic within these cases.

50
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,000
 The fall through keyword will automatically execute the next case, even if it doesn't match.

